-- title:
--   Irreducibility of Φ_q over a DVR with uniformiser q
-- statement:
--   Let $A_0$ be a commutative ring which is a domain and a discrete valuation ring, and let $q$ be a prime number. Assume that the maximal ideal of the local ring $A_0$ equals the principal ideal generated by the image of $q$ in $A_0$, that is, $\mathfrak m_{A_0} = (q)$, so that $q$ is a uniformiser of $A_0$. The conclusion is that the $q$-th cyclotomic polynomial $\Phi_q$, formed over the fraction field $\operatorname{Frac}(A_0)$ of $A_0$ (i.e. `Polynomial.cyclotomic q (FractionRing A₀)`), is irreducible as an element of the polynomial ring over $\operatorname{Frac}(A_0)$. Equivalently, $\operatorname{Frac}(A_0)$ contains no subfield-theoretic obstruction to $[\operatorname{Frac}(A_0)(\zeta_q):\operatorname{Frac}(A_0)] = q-1$; note that the hypothesis excludes rings already ramified at $q$ such as $\mathbb Z_q[\zeta_q]$, while $\mathbb Z_{(q)}$, $\mathbb Z_q$ and the Witt vectors of a finite field of characteristic $q$ are covered.
--
--   This is the standard Eisenstein criterion argument for the irreducibility of the prime-index cyclotomic polynomial, here over the fraction field of any discrete valuation ring unramified at $q$ in the sense that $q$ generates the maximal ideal. It is used in the analysis of the $q$-power structures on the full-level and $\Gamma_0$-level modular curve moduli rings, where it guarantees that the Galois group acts transitively on the primitive $q$-th roots of unity and hence that certain classes do not split over the base.
